(7282999, 1134845, 108580, '<td valign="top">Naturally I''m quite partial to emotive photojournalism from wars to famine, and this image has garnered a 7. Social commentary by photography is a powerful tool. I believe however the west has been desensitised to this type of image. The pleading "African" child. I know I am. I''ve witnessed it for over 30 years. From Nat Geo to CNN. The world is indeed a horrid place and the politicians running it into the ground should all trade places with her for a day, or a week. That doesn''t mean one should stop highlighting cases of extreme poverty or natural disasters, even war. I hope that voters vote with their minds on this image and not their displeasure or uneasiness the sight gives them.</td>', 'Naturally I''m quite partial to emotive photojournalism from wars to famine, and this image has garnered a 7. Social commentary by photography is a powerful tool. I believe however the west has been desensitised to this type of image. The pleading "African" child. I know I am. I''ve witnessed it for over 30 years. From Nat Geo to CNN. The world is indeed a horrid place and the politicians running it into the ground should all trade places with her for a day, or a week. That doesn''t mean one should stop highlighting cases of extreme poverty or natural disasters, even war. I hope that voters vote with their minds on this image and not their displeasure or uneasiness the sight gives them.', '2014-11-10 05:15:18.000000', NULL, 1),
(7283000, 1134902, 108580, '<td valign="top">Super image, with the right lines, colours and the obligatory star effect. Possibly quite hard to do considering the rotating nature of the light source. Did the longish exposure highlight a bit of camera shake or is the new 1200px size harder to post process crisp sharp images? (I had difficulty and reprocessed my own entry 3 times to get it where I wanted it sharpness wise versus how my 800px images are processed.) I''m noticing that the 1200px images appear softer than their 800px counterparts. And yes I have a decent IPS LCD to be able to see the image at it''s best. 8.</td>', 'Super image, with the right lines, colours and the obligatory star effect. Possibly quite hard to do considering the rotating nature of the light source. Did the longish exposure highlight a bit of camera shake or is the new 1200px size harder to post process crisp sharp images? (I had difficulty and reprocessed my own entry 3 times to get it where I wanted it sharpness wise versus how my 800px images are processed.) I''m noticing that the 1200px images appear softer than their 800px counterparts. And yes I have a decent IPS LCD to be able to see the image at it''s best. 8.', '2014-11-10 05:24:14.000000', NULL, 1),
